---
import Base from '../../layouts/Base.astro';
import ArticleCTA from '../../components/ArticleCTA.astro';

const schema = {
  '@context': 'https://schema.org',
  '@type': 'TechArticle',
  '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/emergency-loop-mode#article',
  headline: 'CDJ Emergency Loop Mode - What It Is and What to Do',
  description: 'Your CDJ threw itself into an emergency loop mid-track. What Emergency Loop actually means, why the player did it, and how to get out clean.',
  author: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  publisher: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/#org' },
  datePublished: '2026-07-24',
  dateModified: '2026-07-27',
  inLanguage: 'en',
  // ARTICLE IMAGE (2026-08-04). Google's Article guidance asks for an image,
  // and without one an Article node is never a candidate for a rich result.
  // This is the article's own share card, generated from its own title by
  // scripts/gen-og.mjs, so it represents this article and nothing else.
  // mainEntityOfPage is an @id reference rather than a bare URL string so it
  // resolves to the WebPage node Base.astro emits, instead of being a URL
  // that merely looks like one.
  mainEntityOfPage: { '@id': 'https://www.savemygig.com/fix/emergency-loop-mode#webpage' },
  image: 'https://www.savemygig.com/og/emergency-loop-mode.png',
};
---
<Base
  title="CDJ Emergency Loop Mode: What to Do | Save My Gig"
  description="Your CDJ threw itself into an emergency loop mid-track. What Emergency Loop actually means, why the player did it, and how to get out clean."
  path="/fix/emergency-loop-mode"
  ogImage="/og/emergency-loop-mode.png"
>
  <script type="application/ld+json" slot="head" set:html={JSON.stringify(schema)} />
  <main class="wrap wrap-wide">
    <div class="status-line red">Emergency_Loop</div>
    <h1 style="margin-bottom:1.5rem">CDJ <span class="danger">EMERGENCY LOOP</span>:<br />WHY IT HAPPENS, HOW TO EXIT</h1>

    <div class="panel panel-danger">
      <!-- Official trigger per the CDJ-3000X manual (DRI1956B): "The unit
           automatically plays a Beat Loop if it can't start playing the next
           track when the end of the current track is reached." The drive-health
           reading below is our booth interpretation of the read-failure case. -->
      <p style="margin:0"><strong>Short version:</strong> the player reached the end of the audio it could play and looped the last beats instead of going silent. If you simply let the track run out with nothing loaded, that is the designed behaviour. If it fired while your next track was ready, or mid-track, the player could not read ahead, usually because the drive is struggling.</p>
    </div>

        <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">Why it happens</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>The USB is slow, failing, or was disconnected mid-read, the player ran out of buffered audio.</li>
      <li>A corrupt file: the track's data ends before the track does.</li>
      <li>Rarely: an overloaded drive full of huge WAVs on an older player's USB port.</li>
    </ul>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">In the moment</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li><strong>Don't yank the USB.</strong> The loop is keeping sound in the room.</li>
      <li>Load your next track on the <strong>other player</strong> (from its own source if possible) and mix out of the loop.</li>
      <li>Once you're safe on the other deck: eject properly and watch that drive, one unexplained emergency loop is a warning, two is a dying drive.</li>
      <li>If the same track always triggers it, the file is corrupt: re-download or re-export that track.</li>
    </ul>

    
      </div>
    </details>

    <details class="acc-sec" name="fix-acc">
      <summary><h2 class="acc-h2">After the gig</h2><span class="acc-chev" aria-hidden="true"></span></summary>
      <div class="acc-body">
    <ul class="checklist">
      <li>Test the drive on a computer; copy everything off it while it still reads.</li>
      <li>Full-format (FAT32 + MBR) and re-export, or retire the drive if the format struggles. <a href="/gear">Reliable replacements →</a></li>
      <li>Emergency loops are a symptom. The disease is a single point of failure - <a href="/prepare">fix the system →</a></li>
    </ul>
      </div>
    </details>



    <ArticleCTA article="emergency_loop" />
  </main>
</Base>
